WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:07.160
THOMAS E. PATTERSON: In the words of historian Louis Hartz,

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.590
the United States was born free.

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:12.140
Its liberation from British rule began where

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:15.775
I'm standing, on the village green in Lexington, Massachusetts.

00:00:15.775 --> 00:00:16.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.540
It was here, on a spring morning in 1775,

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:24.080
that American colonists first fought the British.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.530
Paul Revere had ridden through the night to warn local militia that the British

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:30.630
were marching their way.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:34.420
The militia men were farmers, merchants, and craftsman.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:36.560
They numbered less than 100.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:41.770
The British force was 10 times larger, and they were regular soldiers-- part

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:44.260
of the world's finest army.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:45.810
It was no contest.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.010
The firing stopped within moments.

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:49.950
Eight Americans lay dead.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:54.280
The only British casualty was a lightly wounded soldier.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:58.540
But the battle came to be known as "the shot heard round the world."

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:03.250
Democracy had found its champion-- a people who were willing to fight,

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:06.090
to free themselves of a king.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:11.800
From here, the British force marched on Concord, seven miles to the west.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.850
There they faced off against a larger group of local militia.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:19.370
This time, men on both sides fell.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:22.640
As the British marched the 20 miles back to Boston,

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.310
they were fired upon by Americans positioned in the woods.

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:30.360
More than a thousand Americans, one of them 80 years of age,

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.270
had grabbed their muskets and joined the fight.

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:36.470
The British were trained to fight in the open field.

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:40.170
Not against men protected by rocks and trees.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:43.040
Soon the British were in full retreat.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:48.120
When they finally reached Boston, they had lost nearly 100 men-- double

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.690
that of the Americans.

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:53.870
On that April day, more than two centuries ago,

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:57.140
Americans fought for a different form of government.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:00.530
One where the people would govern themselves.

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:04.070
That vision was idealized a year later in the words

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:06.470
of the Declaration of Independence.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:09.699
"We hold these truths to be self-evident,

00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:14.110
that all men are created equal, that they are endowed by their Creator

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:19.460
with certain unalienable rights, that, among these, are life, liberty,

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:21.180
and the pursuit of happiness."

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:24.603
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.310
The ideas that sparked the American Revolution

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:47.390
are today the foundation of America's political culture.

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:52.150
Political culture is a term that refers to the deep seated and widely shared

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:53.800
beliefs of the people.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:56.350
Beliefs that are so much a part of their thinking,

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:58.570
that they're almost second nature.

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:01.430
"Habits of the heart," is how the French writer Alexis de

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:04.480
Tocqueville described such beliefs.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:09.170
Now a nation's political culture gives its politics a distinctive character.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:13.600
Roughly half the world's nations today are classified as democracies.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:16.050
Yet, as a result of cultural differences,

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:18.830
they differ markedly in how they're governed.

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:21.850
German politics is not the same as Greek politics

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:26.570
anymore than Japanese politics is like the politics of India.

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:30.680
In this session, we'll examine the American political culture.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:33.170
We'll explore the origins and distinctiveness

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:35.990
of Americans' political ideals.

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:40.380
We'll talk also about the endurance and the challenges of those ideals.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:44.130
And then we'll conclude with examples of how these beliefs affect

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:46.900
contemporary politics.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:49.320
Now Americans' cultural beliefs originated

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:54.240
in the nation's colonial period, which culminated in the American Revolution.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.960
The revolution was a rejection of an old way of governing for what, at the time,

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:01.176
was a radically new way.

00:04:01.176 --> 00:04:04.820
As James Madison, the nation's fourth president said,

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:09.810
"We were testing whether governments do better without kings then with them. "

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:13.380
But the revolution was more than a question of whether ordinary people

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:15.760
were capable of governing themselves.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:20.000
It reflected a far-ranging change in how people thought.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.090
In the Europe that American settlers had left behind,

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:25.940
power was in the hands of a small few.

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:29.000
Hereditary kings sat atop the political ladder.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.570
Ordinary people were their subjects, possessing few opportunities and even

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:35.240
fewer rights.

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:37.930
It was an age-old system, and the British king

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:41.130
tried to impose it on the American colonies.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:42.570
It didn't hold.

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:47.970
The American continent was too big and too uncharted to be tightly governed.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:51.780
In today's high-tech world, it's hard to imagine what the settlers found

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:53.960
upon arriving in the new world.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:58.810
The seemingly endless tracks of harsh and uncharted wilderness land.

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:02.140
That untamed expanse gradually changed how people thought

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:05.420
about themselves and about government.

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:08.080
Why should they surrender their liberty to a king

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:12.480
when personal freedom was as close as the next wilderness area?

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:15.820
Why should they bow to a king when greater equality could

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:19.080
be had by moving to the next frontier?

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:21.630
Why should they work in the fields of a king?

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:24.680
Why should they accept the age-old feudal system,

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:29.960
when land was freely available and could be made productive by their own hand?

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:34.520
And why should they be governed by laws created by a king an ocean away,

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:39.190
when they knew their governing needs far better than any distant ruler?

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:42.170
It was the gradual dawning of these radical beliefs

00:05:42.170 --> 00:05:45.600
that constituted the real American Revolution.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:48.730
As the nation's second President John Adams said,

00:05:48.730 --> 00:05:53.850
"The American Revolution started first in the minds and hearts of the people."

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:56.710
The armed revolution served to crystallize

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:01.360
what Americans had come to believe-- that they should be free, equal,

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:04.770
self-reliant, and self-governing.

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:07.680
Today, the beliefs nurtured during the colonial period

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:11.500
are the cornerstone of the American political culture.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:15.240
They define even what it means to be an American.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:19.770
The people of most countries derive their identity from shared ancestry.

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:24.820
What makes someone French or Chinese, or defines them as a Turk or a Russian?

00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:26.570
It's common ancestry.

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:30.200
They share land and a bloodline that goes back centuries.

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:33.800
The French have a nation that's more than 1,000 years old.

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:36.090
The Chinese have one that's even older.

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:39.280
It dates back more than 4,000 years.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:40.910
Americans are different.

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:42.860
They don't have a common bloodline.

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:45.130
They come from every corner of the globe--

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:51.020
England, Mexico, Ireland, India, Vietnam, Africa, Germany, you name it.

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:54.010
I was at a concert recently, and struck up a conversation

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:56.660
with the American next to me.

00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:02.060
As it turned out, he was born in Chad, a country in central Africa.

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:07.540
So what, in the absence of shared ancestry, is Americans' common bond?

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:11.950
What do they share, besides the fact they live in the same country?

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:16.070
Writing in the late 19th century, the British historian, James Bryce,

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:19.160
said that Americans' identity stems from the ideals that

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:21.780
emerged during the colonial period.

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:25.710
Bryce was struck by the fact that wherever he traveled in the US,

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:28.340
people voiced the nation's founding ideals.

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:31.370
At the time, the nation was still expanding westward--

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:34.600
a frontier experience that further ingrained the beliefs that

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:36.790
had sparked the revolution.

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:41.540
Even today, these ideals-- liberty, equality, individualism,

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:45.620
self-government-- are central to Americans' identity.

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:49.490
Collectively, they've been called the American creed.

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:54.020
They have been imprinted in Americans' minds by rituals, such as 4th of July

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:57.230
celebrations and the reciting of the Pledge of Allegiance

00:07:57.230 --> 00:08:00.770
by America's children at the start of each school day.

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:05.390
They have also been sustained over time by the words of America's leaders.

00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:09.640
Abraham Lincoln's call for "government of the people, by the people,

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:11.320
and for the people."

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:13.620
Franklin Roosevelt's proclamation that, "We

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:16.820
are a nation of many nationalities, bound together

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:20.010
by the unity of freedom and equality."

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:22.800
Barack Obama's plea in his first inaugural

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:27.430
that America reaffirm the promise that, "All are equal, all are free,

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:32.890
and all deserve a chance to pursue their full measure of happiness."

00:08:32.890 --> 00:08:36.450
But Americans' ongoing embrace of the nation's founding ideals

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:39.679
is more than a matter of rituals and rhetoric.

00:08:39.679 --> 00:08:44.020
Americans have found that their nation's founding ideals are worth keeping.

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:47.000
To be free of repressive government.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:49.000
To be the equal of others.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:50.910
To be economically independent.

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:53.630
To have a say in your own governing.

00:08:53.630 --> 00:08:56.920
Those are abiding principles.

00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:01.440
High deals don't come with a guarantee that people will live up to them.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:05.430
The clearest proof of that, in the American case, is slavery.

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:09.200
For 250 years, black Americans could be bought and sold,

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:11.920
subject to the dictates of their masters.

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:15.290
And even when freed by the Civil War, black Americans

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:20.080
continued to suffer second class status, denied access in the south

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:25.000
to whites-only schools, hospitals, restaurants, and hotels.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:30.070
Unequal treatment was also the fate of Native Americans and of women.

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:31.840
Asians too.

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:37.080
For a long period, America made it clear that Asians were not wanted here.

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:39.780
Chinese and Japanese laborers had been brought here

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:44.080
to do the work of laying the tracks for the Western railroads.

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:47.520
Once the work was done, they were pressured to leave.

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:49.020
Many did.

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:51.170
Later, they were barred from entry.

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:56.340
Through the Immigration Act of 1924, Congress imposed a complete ban

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:58.380
on Asian immigration.

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:02.380
Not just Chinese and Japanese, but Koreans, Indians,

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:04.810
and other Asians as well.

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:08.690
Racism was behind these policies, but America's leaders

00:10:08.690 --> 00:10:12.450
found it more convenient to say that the Asian way of life

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:15.650
didn't conform to the American way.

00:10:15.650 --> 00:10:20.620
In justifying the 1924 ban, President Calvin Coolidge said that,

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:24.660
"Those who do not want to be partakers of the American spirit ought not

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:29.960
to settle in America. " He delivered those words while speaking from

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:33.340
the base of the Statue of Liberty.

00:10:33.340 --> 00:10:36.740
Such examples might suggest that America's cultural ideals

00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:38.400
are devoid of meaning.

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:41.620
High sounding words, but little else.

00:10:41.620 --> 00:10:43.700
And that's sometimes been true.

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:46.250
Cultural values are not commandments.

00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:50.500
They are principles that people can live up to, or ignore.

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:54.190
Americans' cultural beliefs are particularly challenging.

00:10:54.190 --> 00:10:56.230
They are rooted in high ideals.

00:10:56.230 --> 00:10:58.090
Liberty, for instance, is not license.

00:10:58.090 --> 00:11:00.980
It's not freedom to do whatever you please.

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:05.090
It's routed in free choice, but it entails responsibility.

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:09.650
Freedom up to the point where it infringes on the freedom of others.

00:11:09.650 --> 00:11:12.880
Americans' cultural beliefs also conflict.

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:17.020
Self-government, for example, implies the right of the majority to rule.

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:20.110
Whereas liberty implies that individuals have rights

00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:22.830
beyond the reach of the majority.

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:26.540
When majority rule and minority rights come into conflict,

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:30.290
each side can say that it has Americans' ideals on its side,

00:11:30.290 --> 00:11:33.340
and no resort to logic can persuade either side

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:36.630
that the other has the larger claim.

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:40.740
Yet, despite their conflicting implications and unfilled promise,

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:44.560
Americans' ideals have left their mark on the nation's politics.

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:48.100
It's impossible to understand the course of American history

00:11:48.100 --> 00:11:52.070
without taking its cultural ideals into account.

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:55.400
It's true, for example, that racial, gender, ethnic,

00:11:55.400 --> 00:11:58.790
and other forms of discrimination constitute the darkest chapters

00:11:58.790 --> 00:12:00.570
in the nation's history.

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:04.810
Yet it's also true that the struggle of Americans, across the generations,

00:12:04.810 --> 00:12:09.820
to build a more equal society, is one of the nation's brightest chapters.

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.250
How, other than a commitment to equality,

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:14.560
can we explain the frequent political movements

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:18.420
that have sought to make America a more equal place?

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:20.390
There have been scores of such movements,

00:12:20.390 --> 00:12:23.570
including the abolitionist movement, the suffragettes movement, the women's

00:12:23.570 --> 00:12:27.530
rights movement, and the contemporary gay rights movement.

00:12:27.530 --> 00:12:30.550
Particularly noteworthy is the black civil rights movement

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:33.430
of the 1950's and 1960's.

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:36.410
America's mistreatment of blacks came into sharp focus

00:12:36.410 --> 00:12:38.560
with the end of World War II.

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:42.990
Black soldiers had fought bravely to defeat Nazi racism in Europe,

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:46.130
only to return home to racism.

00:12:46.130 --> 00:12:50.750
Black Americans began to agitate for their rights in evermore forceful ways,

00:12:50.750 --> 00:12:55.240
eventually turning to boycotts, sit-ins, rallies, marches.

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:59.910
A major breakthrough came in 1954, when the US Supreme Court invalidated

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:02.760
state laws, in place throughout the South,

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:06.640
that required black children to attend public schools separate from those

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:08.250
of white children.

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:12.820
In its ruling, the court said, "Separate educational facilities

00:13:12.820 --> 00:13:15.520
are inherently unequal."

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:19.470
As the civil rights movement grew, led by inspirational leaders like Martin

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:24.930
Luther King and Ralph Abernathy, it attracted ever broader white support.

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:30.270
By the early 1960's, polls indicated that 3/4 of non-Southern whites

00:13:30.270 --> 00:13:33.560
favored an end to racial discrimination in restaurants, hotels,

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:36.370
and other public accommodations.

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:42.130
In 1964, Congress passed the Civil Rights Act, which did precisely that.

00:13:42.130 --> 00:13:45.520
As he prepared to sign the legislation, President Lyndon Johnson

00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:49.630
said, "We believe that all men are created equal.

00:13:49.630 --> 00:13:52.380
Yet many are denied equal treatment.

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:56.340
We believe that all men are entitled to the blessings of liberty.

00:13:56.340 --> 00:14:00.310
Yet millions are being deprived of those blessings-- not because

00:14:00.310 --> 00:14:04.490
of their own failures, but because of the color of their skin.

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:05.900
But it cannot continue.

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:08.560
The principles of our freedom forbid it.

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:12.280
And the law I will sign tonight forbids it."

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:14.650
The civil rights movement would not have succeeded

00:14:14.650 --> 00:14:18.730
without the courage and determination of the black community.

00:14:18.730 --> 00:14:21.660
But the movement also had behind it the moral force

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:25.000
of Americans' professed belief in equality.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:27.480
It was that force that Martin Luther King

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:31.830
summoned when he challenged America to "Rise up and live out

00:14:31.830 --> 00:14:37.630
the true meaning of its creed, that all men are created equal."

00:14:37.630 --> 00:14:40.670
As I noted earlier, a nation's political culture

00:14:40.670 --> 00:14:43.860
gives its politics a distinctive form.

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:46.210
Cultural beliefs clearly do not determine

00:14:46.210 --> 00:14:48.480
precisely what a people will do.

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:51.320
But, in the long run, they affect what a people

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:54.280
will regard as appropriate and desirable,

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:56.860
and thus what they will try to achieve.

00:14:56.860 --> 00:15:00.220
Cultural beliefs serve as unstated rules of behavior.

00:15:00.220 --> 00:15:03.940
They act as boundaries on what people will find acceptable

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:06.390
and what they will strive for.

00:15:06.390 --> 00:15:09.610
Take, for example, Americans belief in individualism.

00:15:09.610 --> 00:15:13.280
Although individualism is also a part of the political culture

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:16.830
of European nations, it takes exaggerated form

00:15:16.830 --> 00:15:19.960
in the United States because of its unique history.

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:22.210
Americans heightened sense of individualism

00:15:22.210 --> 00:15:25.890
can be seen in the results of a recent cross-national survey that

00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:30.350
was conducted in four European countries and the United States.

00:15:30.350 --> 00:15:32.560
Respondents in each of these countries were

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:36.360
asked, "What is more important-- that nobody is in need

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:40.790
or that individuals have the freedom to pursue life's goals?"

00:15:40.790 --> 00:15:43.990
As you can see from the figure, Americans responded differently

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:46.920
to this question then did Europeans.

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:51.450
Most of the European respondents ranked helping those in need more highly.

00:15:51.450 --> 00:15:57.120
In contrast, most American respondents placed individual freedom at the top.

00:15:57.120 --> 00:16:00.210
Not all American respondents ranked individual pursuits

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:02.450
ahead of helping the needy, but the majority

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:06.980
did-- the reverse of the pattern for Europeans.

00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:09.720
Now if this difference was simply one of opinion,

00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:13.070
it would be interesting, but not of great consequence.

00:16:13.070 --> 00:16:16.280
In fact, the difference has consequences.

00:16:16.280 --> 00:16:20.360
Consistent with its greater cultural emphasis on individualism, the United

00:16:20.360 --> 00:16:24.510
States, though it has a higher poverty rate then do European countries,

00:16:24.510 --> 00:16:27.450
spends less on programs for the poor.

00:16:27.450 --> 00:16:29.910
About 25% less.

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:33.070
The rules of welfare eligibility also differ.

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:36.200
In most European countries, for example, citizens

00:16:36.200 --> 00:16:39.440
are entitled to government-provided health insurance.

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:43.480
They don't have to prove that they're poor to be eligible for it.

00:16:43.480 --> 00:16:46.470
In the United States, proof is required.

00:16:46.470 --> 00:16:49.670
Individuals must show that they cannot buy it on their own--

00:16:49.670 --> 00:16:53.480
that they lack the financial means-- before they're eligible for government

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:56.420
help in obtaining health insurance.

00:16:56.420 --> 00:17:00.090
Americans are not necessarily less sympathetic with the poor.

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:04.020
Compared with Europeans, they are twice as likely to give to charities.

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:07.660
But Americans are less inclined to enact welfare policies that

00:17:07.660 --> 00:17:09.609
could weaken self-reliance.

00:17:09.609 --> 00:17:12.609
From America's individualistic perspective,

00:17:12.609 --> 00:17:16.140
people are obligated to work hard and take care of themselves

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:20.950
unless they have a verified need for government help.

00:17:20.950 --> 00:17:23.319
Public education provides another example

00:17:23.319 --> 00:17:28.270
of how America's cultural values affect its policy choices.

00:17:28.270 --> 00:17:32.540
The United States has easily the world's largest higher education system--

00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:35.920
more than 4,000 colleges and universities.

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:39.590
Most of them are public institutions, created by government

00:17:39.590 --> 00:17:43.630
and supported, in substantial part, by government funding.

00:17:43.630 --> 00:17:46.730
The United States also spends more per pupil for primary

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:50.920
and secondary public education than does nearly every other country.

00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:55.730
The European average per student is slightly more than $9,000 annually.

00:17:55.730 --> 00:17:59.650
The United States spends about $12,000 per student.

00:17:59.650 --> 00:18:00.970
Now why is that?

00:18:00.970 --> 00:18:05.680
Why does the United States invest so heavily in public education?

00:18:05.680 --> 00:18:08.780
Its political culture is among the reasons.

00:18:08.780 --> 00:18:11.860
If individualism is to be upheld, you have

00:18:11.860 --> 00:18:15.880
to give people the means-- a proper education to succeed.

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:19.690
And if equality is to be upheld, educational opportunities

00:18:19.690 --> 00:18:24.720
have to be plentiful, available to the many rather than to the few.

00:18:24.720 --> 00:18:28.910
Equality of opportunity, giving every child a chance to succeed,

00:18:28.910 --> 00:18:33.750
is the guiding principle of America's system of public education.

00:18:33.750 --> 00:18:37.230
When that system began to take root two centuries ago,

00:18:37.230 --> 00:18:39.700
it was called the great equalizer.

00:18:39.700 --> 00:18:42.950
It sought to give children of widely different backgrounds

00:18:42.950 --> 00:18:47.360
a somewhat equal start in life so that each could succeed or fail

00:18:47.360 --> 00:18:49.060
on their own merits.

00:18:49.060 --> 00:18:53.040
Writing in the late 19th century, the socialist Leon Samson

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:56.820
noted the stark difference between the philosophy of public education

00:18:56.820 --> 00:18:58.710
in the United States and Europe.

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:01.930
Said Sampson, "The European ruling class were

00:19:01.930 --> 00:19:04.700
open in their contempt for working people.

00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:09.040
But in the United States, equality, and liberty were extolled in the public

00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:13.880
schools. " Sampson concluded that American schools embodied a unique

00:19:13.880 --> 00:19:15.930
conception of equality.

00:19:15.930 --> 00:19:18.900
Everyone was being trained in much the same way,

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:23.640
so that each person would at least have a chance at success.

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:26.240
Since then, of course, European nations have broadened

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:28.590
the reach of their education systems.

00:19:28.590 --> 00:19:31.460
Nevertheless, differences remain.

00:19:31.460 --> 00:19:33.800
In some European systems, high school students

00:19:33.800 --> 00:19:37.120
are separated into those that receive pre-college training

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:39.550
and those that get vocational training.

00:19:39.550 --> 00:19:45.500
In contrast, most American schools offer are relatively standardized curriculum.

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:49.970
Each student takes more or less the same set of courses-- math, science,

00:19:49.970 --> 00:19:52.480
language, and the like.

00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:56.790
Some American educators have questioned the wisdom of this approach.

00:19:56.790 --> 00:20:00.320
Given the differences in students' interests and aptitude,

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:02.400
they think vocational training should be a larger

00:20:02.400 --> 00:20:05.570
part of the US educational system.

00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:10.220
But a standardized education is consistent with America's core values.

00:20:10.220 --> 00:20:15.300
It's squarely in line with the principle of equal opportunity.

00:20:15.300 --> 00:20:18.350
I could give your other examples-- everything from home ownership

00:20:18.350 --> 00:20:21.570
to gun laws, that illustrate the impact of America's

00:20:21.570 --> 00:20:25.900
political culture on its policies-- but the point would be the same.

00:20:25.900 --> 00:20:29.730
Any assessment of US politics must take into account

00:20:29.730 --> 00:20:32.400
the influence of its cultural values.

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:35.150
It's far from the only important factor, as we'll

00:20:35.150 --> 00:20:40.380
see in subsequent sessions of this course, but it's one of them.

00:20:40.380 --> 00:20:42.880
Before summarising what's been said in this session,

00:20:42.880 --> 00:20:47.230
I'd like to speak briefly about the nature of politics.

00:20:47.230 --> 00:20:51.740
Political scientist Harold Lasswell described politics as the struggle over

00:20:51.740 --> 00:20:55.350
"Who gets what, when, and how. " In other words,

00:20:55.350 --> 00:21:00.370
"Politics is the process through which a society settles it's conflicts,"

00:21:00.370 --> 00:21:05.990
over who gets the benefits and who pays the costs of public policy.

00:21:05.990 --> 00:21:10.350
There are two basic reasons why conflict is at the center of politics.

00:21:10.350 --> 00:21:11.730
One is scarcity.

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:14.240
Even the richest nations do not have enough wealth

00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:17.380
to satisfy everyone's demands.

00:21:17.380 --> 00:21:21.500
That sets up conflict over how a nation's resources are distributed.

00:21:21.500 --> 00:21:25.230
Everyone wants a piece of the pie, but the pie is not big enough

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:27.870
to give everyone what they want.

00:21:27.870 --> 00:21:30.710
Consider, for example, the difference in the quality

00:21:30.710 --> 00:21:32.750
of America's public schools.

00:21:32.750 --> 00:21:36.310
Wealthy suburban districts have better school facilities then

00:21:36.310 --> 00:21:39.210
do poor inner city neighborhoods.

00:21:39.210 --> 00:21:42.950
The reason for this is that public schools are financed, in large part,

00:21:42.950 --> 00:21:44.460
at the local level.

00:21:44.460 --> 00:21:48.420
Roughly half of school funding is provided by local taxes,

00:21:48.420 --> 00:21:52.110
and wealthier communities have a stronger tax base.

00:21:52.110 --> 00:21:55.010
In an effort to improve their schools, poorer districts

00:21:55.010 --> 00:21:59.480
have pressure state governments to equalize spending across districts.

00:21:59.480 --> 00:22:03.720
This proposal has been strongly opposed by wealthier districts, which

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:06.620
fear it will weaken their schools.

00:22:06.620 --> 00:22:10.090
Such conflicts, stemming from insufficient resources

00:22:10.090 --> 00:22:14.300
to satisfy everyone's demands, are an everyday part of politics.

00:22:14.300 --> 00:22:17.850
Nearly any time that money, status, or other type of resources

00:22:17.850 --> 00:22:22.660
that issue, people will fight over its distribution.

00:22:22.660 --> 00:22:26.830
The second major source of conflict in society is values.

00:22:26.830 --> 00:22:29.900
More precisely, values that diverge.

00:22:29.900 --> 00:22:33.960
One person's beliefs about what's right and wrong may differ radically from

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:35.470
another person's.

00:22:35.470 --> 00:22:39.080
When such value differences exist around an issue of policy,

00:22:39.080 --> 00:22:42.120
conflict is almost inevitable.

00:22:42.120 --> 00:22:44.240
A case in point is the abortion issue.

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:47.950
Ever since the Supreme Court, in 1973, declared

00:22:47.950 --> 00:22:50.460
that women have a right to choose abortion,

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:53.640
the issue has been a persistent source of conflict.

00:22:53.640 --> 00:22:56.960
To some, abortion is a woman's right to choose.

00:22:56.960 --> 00:23:01.510
To others, abortion violates the rights of the unborn.

00:23:01.510 --> 00:23:05.060
These are opposing beliefs, and largely incompatible.

00:23:05.060 --> 00:23:09.820
One side would ban abortion, the other would leave the choice to the woman.

00:23:09.820 --> 00:23:12.010
How do you bridge that difference?

00:23:12.010 --> 00:23:13.790
Well, you don't do it easily.

00:23:13.790 --> 00:23:17.220
Ever since the Supreme Court's 1973 ruling,

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:21.410
abortion foes and supporters have been fighting the issue in the courts,

00:23:21.410 --> 00:23:25.020
through street demonstrations, and in the halls of Congress and state

00:23:25.020 --> 00:23:26.570
legislatures.

00:23:26.570 --> 00:23:29.730
Such conflicts are an everyday part of politics.

00:23:29.730 --> 00:23:35.810
Whenever people's values clash, each side will fight to come out on top.

00:23:35.810 --> 00:23:39.390
Who wins out when there is conflict over resources or values?

00:23:39.390 --> 00:23:42.580
Which side gets what it wants?

00:23:42.580 --> 00:23:44.770
The abstract answer is that the winners are

00:23:44.770 --> 00:23:47.750
those with the most political power.

00:23:47.750 --> 00:23:50.760
Power is a basic concept of politics.

00:23:50.760 --> 00:23:53.470
Political scientists define power as the ability

00:23:53.470 --> 00:23:57.440
of an actor-- such as an individual, group, organization,

00:23:57.440 --> 00:24:02.550
or institution-- to influence policy or control the behavior of others.

00:24:02.550 --> 00:24:06.560
Influence and control-- those are the marks of power.

00:24:06.560 --> 00:24:12.170
Actors with enough power can impose or cut taxes, prohibit or permit abortion,

00:24:12.170 --> 00:24:16.020
increase or reduce welfare benefits, you name it.

00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:18.520
Given what power can do, it's not surprising

00:24:18.520 --> 00:24:22.250
that much of what happens in politics centers on power.

00:24:22.250 --> 00:24:26.510
Trying to get it, as in the case of campaigning for election to office,

00:24:26.510 --> 00:24:31.620
and trying to use it, as in the case of lobbying to influence legislation.

00:24:31.620 --> 00:24:34.810
In subsequent sessions, we'll examine the distribution of power

00:24:34.810 --> 00:24:37.200
in the American political system.

00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:40.800
Who has power in America, and who doesn't.

00:24:40.800 --> 00:24:42.550
That's one of the most important questions

00:24:42.550 --> 00:24:47.820
to ask and answer about any political system.

00:24:47.820 --> 00:24:51.020
OK, let's wrap up what we've said in this session.

00:24:51.020 --> 00:24:55.200
We noted that Americans' core beliefs, their political culture,

00:24:55.200 --> 00:24:57.960
originated in the conditions of the New World,

00:24:57.960 --> 00:25:01.870
particularly its vast tracts of wilderness land.

00:25:01.870 --> 00:25:03.920
It opened the minds of colonial Americans

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:06.740
to a new way of governing-- one rooted in beliefs

00:25:06.740 --> 00:25:12.270
about personal liberty, individualism, equality, and self-government.

00:25:12.270 --> 00:25:15.090
We also noted that these ideals have been passed along

00:25:15.090 --> 00:25:17.880
to each succeeding generation of Americans,

00:25:17.880 --> 00:25:22.050
insuring their continuing influence on the nation's politics.

00:25:22.050 --> 00:25:25.200
Americans have argued over the meaning of these ideals,

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:27.970
but not whether they're worth keeping.

00:25:27.970 --> 00:25:30.660
We pointed out some of the challenges that Americans

00:25:30.660 --> 00:25:32.990
face in living up to their ideals.

00:25:32.990 --> 00:25:36.700
Challenges the stem in part from the demanding and sometimes conflicting

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:39.080
nature of these ideals.

00:25:39.080 --> 00:25:42.940
We then looked at how these beliefs affect public policy,

00:25:42.940 --> 00:25:47.300
pointing out that they set boundaries on what people will regard as desirable,

00:25:47.300 --> 00:25:51.220
and thus what they will try to achieve politically.

00:25:51.220 --> 00:25:54.010
Finally, we noted that politics is the process

00:25:54.010 --> 00:25:58.820
by which society settles it's conflicts over scarce resources and opposing

00:25:58.820 --> 00:26:00.370
values.

